{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfrwnzuvm6Hgux1VaZfry","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed `next_run` in `sched/core.py`, and `tests/test_sched.py` now passes (1 passed). I also checked the DST case by hand with one call (Berlin, 09:00, starting from 2026-03-28 12:00 UTC). It returned 2026-03-29 09:00+02:00, which is the correct local time after the change.\n\nThere were two root causes:\n\n1. **Skipped day after 22:00 UTC:** the function took today's date from `now` in UTC, not in the target zone. At 23:30 UTC it was already June 2 in Berlin, so the candidate was built for the wrong date. Since 01:00 on that UTC date had passed, it added a day on top of that.\n2. **One hour off around DST:** the \"next day\" step added 24 hours in UTC. Across the March change a local day is 23 hours long, so the result landed an hour off the requested wall-clock time.\n\nThe fix takes the date from `now` converted to `tz`. It then builds the candidate from the `hour:minute` fields on that date, so the DST offset is applied per day. If that isn't strictly after `now`, it moves to the next calendar day and builds it the same way. The signature is unchanged.\n\nIf the requested time falls in the spring-forward gap (for example 02:30 on the change day in Berlin), the result shifts forward to the next valid instant. I didn't add a test for the gap or the March change, only the manual check above."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3530,"cache_read_input_tokens":28578,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3530},"output_tokens":461,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e38dca28-b7ce-4242-94cb-265b613fa932","uuid":"249f9a62-1daa-4889-96c4-53b327f38